  "body": "Part of #2574 (research answer 1, option a).\n\nMaintainer request, 2026-11-18, in his words (quoted in #2574):\n> \"That can generate a \"loose\" model from the machine file. That would means, table, column, axis, head, spindle etc etc. All joints. \"Defined\" and loosly generated as a generic block and cylinders design.\"\n\n## Scope\n- Add a module `machine_model` in `camcraft-sim`. Its input is plain data of its own (parts, parent, joint kind, axis, travel, spindle body). `camcraft-sim` gets no edge to `camcraft-post`: the caller maps `MachineTree` (#2583) into the input.\n- Make one closed body for each part: a box for a table, saddle or column, a cylinder for a spindle body, sized from `travel`, `spindle_d`, `spindle_len` and the tool station.\n- Pose each body for a set of axis values along the kinematic chain (linear and rotary joints).\n- Mark each body as \"generated\". A check that uses it says \"judged on a generated model\".\n- A part with no number to size it gets no body and a named note. It never gets a guessed size without the note.\n- Degenerate input (non-finite, zero travel) gives a clean error, never a panic.\n\n## Done means\nTests pin the bodies and their poses for a 3-axis VMC and the tilt-head example at two axis positions each, the \"generated\" mark, and the refusal of degenerate input.\n\nDepends on #2583 (for the mapping only; the module can land first with its own types).\n",
